```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <iterator>
#include <functional>
#include <stdexcept>

template<typename Compare = std::less<>>
void insertAfter(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& key, const std::string& value, Compare compare = Compare()) {
    if (list.empty()) {
        list.emplace_back(value);
        return;
    }

    auto it = list.begin();
    auto end = list.end();

    while (it != end && !compare(*it, key)) {
        ++it;
    }

    if (it == end) {
        list.emplace_back(value);
        return;
    }

    it.insert_after(*it, value);
}
```

This code defines a function called `insertAfter` that takes a `std::forward_list<std::string>`, two string arguments, and an optional comparison function. The function will insert the second string argument after the first occurrence of the first string argument in the list if it exists; otherwise, the second string will be appended to the end of the list.

The function uses iterators to traverse the forward list and find the specified key using the provided comparison function. If the comparison function is not provided, it defaults to `std::less<>`, which means that the search is case-sensitive. To make the search case-insensitive, you can use a custom comparison function such as:

```cpp
