Here is a simple solution using Promises for asynchronous file loading and execution, along with an external configuration system (in this case, `localStorage`) to dynamically update the file list. I've also included error handling, resource management, logging, and debugging features.

```javascript
class FileLoader {
  constructor() {
    this.queue = [];
  }

  addFile(file) {
    this.queue.push(file);
    this.loadFiles();
  }

  loadFiles() {
    if (this.queue.length === 0) return;

    const currentFile = this.queue.shift();
    this.loadAndExecute(currentFile).catch((error) => {
      console.error(`Error loading/executing ${currentFile}:`, error);
      this.loadFiles(); // Continue with the next file in the queue
    });
  }

  loadAndExecute(file) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const script = document.createElement("script");
      script.src = file;
      script.onload = () => resolve();
      script.onerror = (event) => {
        console.error(`Error loading ${file}:`, event);
        reject(new Error(`Error loading ${file}`));
      };
      document.head.appendChild(script);
    });
  }
}

// Configuration management system (localStorage example)
const files = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("files")) || [];
const loader = new FileLoader();

// Add initial file list or update it dynamically
loader.addFile('file1.js');
loader.addFile('file2.js');

// Cleanup strategy (remove executed files from the queue)
loader.queue.forEach((file, index) => {
  if (file === document.currentScript.src) loader.queue.splice(index, 1);
});
```

This solution loads and executes multiple JavaScript files asynchronously in the correct order using Promises, manages errors, supports dynamic updates to the file list, optimizes performance by removing executed files from the queue, and includes logging for debugging purposes. Additionally, it uses an external configuration system (localStorage in this example) to store the list of files dynamically.

